    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d164">

                <pb facs="0169" n="119" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg119"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1457</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Francis</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-04-22T18:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 22, 1918,
                                6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>77. Your 74, April 5, 6 p.m. Facts as to 500 locomotives and 10,000 cars ordered
                    from American credits as follows: 50 locomotives, Baldwin, at shops; and 50,
                    American Locomotive Co., at Seattle ready for shipment when tonnage available.
                    Two hundred of balance rebuilt for American railroads to be replaced when
                    required for Russia. Remaining 200, option of Russian Government to complete
                    during current year. Ten thousand cars, none shipped. No locomotives <pb
                        facs="0170" n="120" xml:id="pg120"/> or cars will be shipped until certain
                    that development of railways will benefit Russia, not Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
